# Line endings are normalised in the repository, whatever the machine that committed them.
#
# Without this, eighteen Rust and TOML files carried CRLF while the other 173 files carried LF —
# drift from edits made by tools that rewrite a whole file. It is invisible until someone on
# another platform clones the repository and sees a diff they did not make, or until a single
# changed line arrives as a changed file.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` stores LF for everything git decides is text, and checks it out as LF. Windows
# handles LF fine in every tool this project uses — Rust, Node, PowerShell 7, and Git Bash.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Files where the ending is not cosmetic. A `.bat` or `.cmd` with LF endings can fail on older
# Windows shells, and a `.ps1` signed by Authenticode is signed over its exact bytes.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary, so git neither diffs nor normalises them.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.bmp binary
*.woff2 binary
*.exe binary

# Generated. Marking them keeps a lockfile change out of a diff someone has to read, while still
# tracking the file itself.
pnpm-lock.yaml -diff linguist-generated
Cargo.lock -diff linguist-generated

# What GitHub counts as the language of this repository. Without this the vendored font subsets and
# the lockfiles skew the bar at the top of the page.
docs/screenshots/* linguist-documentation
